# Agent-as-a-Service Platform — Project Constitution

You are working on a multi-tenant Agent-as-a-Service (AaaS) SaaS platform.
These rules override any instinct to scaffold quickly. Read them fully before any edit.
This file is the single source of truth. If another document in the tree contradicts
it, this file wins and you report the contradiction.

## 0. CURRENT STATE (as of 2026-11-20; verify, do not assume)
- Stack: FastAPI + SQLAlchemy 2.0 + Postgres + Chroma + Gemini. Frontend is a Vite +
  React + TypeScript dark app in `frontend/`.
- Phase 1 + 2 are functional: API key auth, tenant-scoped documents, tenant-scoped
  chat sessions, retrieval, Gemini chat.
- Phase 3 is the dark web app. Login and invalid-key paths are verified in-browser.
  The signed-in chat screen is NOT yet verified end-to-end.
- This working tree still shares space with an unrelated product: the Velobase
  Harness (Prisma, Next.js, `services/`, several Dockerfiles, its own CI under
  `.github/`). None of it is used by `app/`. Separating the two is build-order step 1,
  IN PROGRESS. The separation happens by building a fresh repository that simply does
  not copy those files — they are not deleted in place.
- Once that fresh repository is the working repo, no harness artifact may ever enter
  it. If you encounter a reference to Prisma, pnpm, Next.js, `velobase`, or a
  `services/` directory, it is a leftover from the old tree: report it, do not build
  on it, do not carry it forward.
- There is NO Dockerfile for this application yet. Local dev uses a gitignored
  `docker-compose.phase1.yml`. Do not invent a deployment pipeline unless asked.
- Six architecture documents written for an abandoned Next.js version of this product
  were removed from the tree because they describe a system that does not exist.
  Do not recreate them or treat any similar document as authoritative.

## 1. THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS MOST
There is ONE agent engine. There is NOT 60 agents' worth of code.

An "agent" is a row of configuration data, not a module, not a folder, not a class.
Adding the 61st agent must require ZERO new application code — only a new config
record and, at most, a new entry in the tool registry.

If you are ever about to create a file like `agents/customer_support_agent.py`,
`agents/real_estate_agent.py`, or a per-agent route, STOP. You have misunderstood
the architecture. The correct move is a new row in `agent_templates`.

## 2. TENANT ISOLATION — NON-NEGOTIABLE
2.1 Every tenant-owned table has `tenant_id UUID NOT NULL` with an index, and a
    composite index on (`tenant_id`, primary lookup column). A `tenants` table owns
    identity; API keys reference a tenant. Ownership is never an untyped string.
2.2 No database query may be written without a tenant filter. Enforce this
    structurally, not by discipline:
    - All data access goes through repository classes that accept a `TenantContext`
      in the constructor, and that build every query with the tenant filter applied.
    - The owner predicate belongs IN THE SQL. Fetching a row by primary key and then
      comparing ownership in the service layer is not acceptable: the row has already
      been read, and a future caller will forget the check.
    - Raw or ad-hoc queries in route handlers and services are forbidden.
    - Where the database supports it, additionally enable Row Level Security driven
      by a session variable set from the authenticated tenant.
2.3 The agent catalog is split into TWO tables. This is the fix for the known bug:
    - `agent_templates` — platform-owned, global, NO tenant_id. Seeded by migration.
      Readable by all tenants. Writable ONLY by a platform-admin role, through a
      separate admin router that tenant API keys cannot reach.
    - `tenant_agents` — tenant-owned instances. Has `tenant_id`, references a
      `template_id`, and stores config overrides, enabled state, and per-tenant
      credentials. Tenants may only CRUD rows whose tenant_id matches their context.
2.4 Slug uniqueness is `UNIQUE(tenant_id, slug)`, never globally unique. A global
    unique slug both blocks legitimate names and leaks the existence of other
    tenants' agents through constraint errors.
2.5 A missing resource and another tenant's resource both return 404, never 403.
    403 confirms existence.
2.6 `tests/test_tenant_isolation.py` asserts, for EVERY tenant-owned resource, that
    tenant A's key receives 404 for tenant B's id. This suite is a merge gate.

## 3. AGENT DEFINITION CONTRACT
Every agent template is described by this schema, validated with Pydantic (and Zod
on the frontend). No agent may exist outside this shape.

{
  "slug": "customer-support",
  "name": "AI Customer Support Agent",
  "category": "support",
  "version": 1,
  "description": "Answers customer questions from company knowledge and escalates hard cases.",
  "system_prompt_template": "You are a support agent for {{brand_name}}. ...",
  "model": { "provider": "gemini", "name": "gemini-2.0-flash", "temperature": 0.3 },
  "knowledge": { "enabled": true, "top_k": 6, "min_score": 0.35, "require_citations": true },
  "tools": ["search_docs", "create_ticket", "escalate_to_human"],
  "memory": { "type": "session", "window": 12 },
  "inputs": [
    { "key": "brand_name", "label": "Brand name", "type": "string", "required": true },
    { "key": "tone", "label": "Tone", "type": "enum", "options": ["formal","friendly"], "default": "friendly" }
  ],
  "guardrails": {
    "refuse_topics": [], "pii_redaction": true, "max_output_tokens": 1024,
    "human_approval_required": false
  },
  "limits": { "max_runs_per_day": 500, "max_tokens_per_run": 8000 }
}

`system_prompt_template` placeholders are filled from the tenant's `inputs` values at
run time. A tenant editing their agent edits input VALUES and permitted overrides —
never the platform's template row.

## 4. ENGINE RUNTIME CONTRACT
A single `AgentRunner.run(tenant_ctx, tenant_agent_id, input, stream=True)` serves
every agent. Pipeline, in order:
  1. Load tenant_agent + template; merge config (template defaults <- tenant overrides).
  2. Check quota and plan limits. Reject early with a typed error.
  3. Create an `agent_runs` row with status `running` BEFORE any model call.
  4. Resolve tools: only those listed in the template AND enabled for the tenant.
  5. Retrieve knowledge if enabled — scoped to this tenant's documents only.
  6. Render the system prompt; assemble messages with the memory window.
  7. Call the model through an `LLMProvider` interface. Gemini is one implementation.
     Adding another provider must not touch the engine.
  8. Run the tool-call loop with a hard max-iteration cap (default 8).
  9. Persist every step to `agent_run_steps`: step type, tool, args, result, tokens,
     latency, error.
 10. Finalize: status, token counts, computed cost, output.
Streaming and non-streaming share this exact path. Never fork the pipeline.

## 5. TOOLS
- A central `ToolRegistry` maps tool name -> handler + JSON schema + required scopes.
- Every tool handler takes `TenantContext` first and may touch only that tenant's data.
- Third-party credentials live in `tenant_integrations`, encrypted at rest with
  envelope encryption; the key comes from the environment and is never stored in the DB.
- Any tool with an external side effect (send email, post publicly, issue a refund,
  write to a CRM) is marked `requires_approval` and by default writes a pending action
  for human confirmation instead of firing.

## 6. OBSERVABILITY, QUOTA, BILLING
- `agent_runs` and `agent_run_steps` are the only source of truth for usage.
- Quota middleware reads from those tables, never from a counter that can drift.
- Each run records tenant_id, tenant_agent_id, tokens_in, tokens_out, cost_usd,
  status, error_code, duration_ms.
- Plan limits (runs/month, agents allowed, document MB, seats) live in a `plans` table
  and are enforced in one place.

## 7. CODE RULES
- FastAPI, async, Pydantic v2, SQLAlchemy 2.0, Alembic.
- SCHEMA CHANGES HAPPEN IN MIGRATIONS ONLY. No `create_all`, no `ALTER` or
  `CREATE INDEX` at application startup. Startup DDL races between instances and makes
  migrations untrustworthy.
- Frontend: keep the existing dark app's stack and design language. Agent pages are
  GENERATED from the agent schema. Never hand-write a page per agent.
- Typed errors with stable machine-readable codes. No bare `except:`. No fallback that
  reports a failure as a success.
- No secrets in code, tests, fixtures, or compose files. `.env.example` lists only the
  variables this application actually reads, with empty values.
- Any operational or metrics endpoint requires an admin credential or is not mounted
  on the public app.
- No new dependency without stating why in the commit message.

## 8. HOW YOU MUST WORK WITH ME
8.1 AUDIT BEFORE BUILD. For every task: read the relevant existing code, report what
    is actually there, state the gap, propose a plan, and WAIT for approval. Never
    write code in the same turn as an audit.
8.2 ONE STEP AT A TIME. Finish one scoped step, show the diff summary and how to
    verify it, then stop. Do not chain steps or phases.
8.3 NEVER CLAIM SOMETHING WORKS UNTIL IT IS EXECUTED. "Should work", "now functional",
    and "verified" are forbidden unless you ran it and are pasting real output.
    Untested work is reported as "written, not verified".
8.4 If you find a second problem while fixing the first, REPORT it. Do not fix it
    silently in the same change.
8.5 Cite `file:line` for every claim about existing behaviour, so I can check it.
8.6 Never run tests, commit over a failing suite, or push without being told.

## 9. KNOWN DEVIATIONS (open; do not rediscover, do not silently fix)
  D1. CRITICAL — agent catalog is not tenant-scoped. All five agent routes accept any
      valid API key and then discard the caller identity (`_ = user_id`). Any tenant
      can read, edit, or deactivate every agent, including the system prompt used by
      every other tenant's chat. Fix is section 2.3.
  D2. Live tables have no `tenant_id`. Ownership is a string `user_id` from the API key.
  D3. Document and session loads select by primary key with no tenant predicate; the
      service compares ownership after the fetch. Violates 2.2.
  D4. `app/main.py` performs DDL at startup. Violates section 7.
  D5. `agents.slug` is globally unique. Violates 2.4.
  D6. `/api/v1/metrics` is mounted with no authentication.
  D7. Test coverage is three health/auth tests. No agent, chat, session, ingest, or
      cross-tenant test exists.

## 10. BUILD ORDER (do not jump ahead)
  1. Clean repository, secrets out, uncommitted work preserved.
  2. Tenant model + `agent_templates`/`tenant_agents` split + scoped repositories +
     isolation test suite. Closes D1–D3, D5.
  3. Alembic adopted; startup DDL removed. Closes D4.
  4. Engine hardening: run tracking, tool registry, provider abstraction, quota.
  5. Agent builder UI generated from the schema.
  6. Seed and verify the first 10 agents end-to-end with real integrations.
  7. Marketplace listing, subscriptions, billing.
  8. Remaining agent categories, in batches, each with a working integration.
An agent is not listed as purchasable until its integrations are real and its
end-to-end test passes. A marketplace of non-functional agents is worse than an
empty one.
